  "preamble": "Devoicing of the voiced bilabial stop [b] to tenuis [p] is a common unconditioned or initial-position change. It occurred across most Xiang and southern Bingzhou Jin varieties of Chinese, where Middle Chinese voiced initials became unaspirated voiceless stops; in Oghuric Turkic (Chuvash pin 'thousand' vs Turkish bin); in Hmongic, where the lost voicing survives as a low tone register; in Kott and Arin among the Yeniseian languages; and in Chamorro, where p is the regular reflex of Proto-Malayo-Polynesian *b. Riang (Palaungic) and the Pearic languages also devoiced *b with a register or tone reflex (Proto-Palaungic *bluːʔ 'thigh' > Riang pluʔ², Proto-Pearic *bluː > Chong plṳː).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Devoicing, Voice Loss, Register Split",
